Z||000008||Patrick's Dad||10-26-2000||07:10 PM||||From what C&D said this past month about the Speedster, it's a far cry from what our butts could take in the long haul.<P>"At about $30,000, the Speedster would face a swarm of competent foes...whose manufacturers are only too happy to offer air conditioning (so what? -me), power windows, spare tires, adjustable passenger seats, real fuel and temp gauges, softtops that are easier to pitch than a tent, and upholstery somewhat thicker than an armadillo's...Americans taller than five feet ten will find their heads in permanent contact with the collapsible softtop, their hips (with) aluminum bulkheads, thier knees (with) third-to-fourth upshifts...any shoe beyond a size 9 is as likely to depress two pedals as one."<P>For me, who has a hulk for winter use and a two car garage, I say, "Yeah, baby!" I'm slightly under 5'10", and, though I wear larger than a size 9 shoe, many things are learned, an "elemental" (also per C&D) car like this appeals to me, but it needs a lot more polish to make it here. Personally, I'd like to see the Cadillac Catera with the LS1 and B/W 6-speed, as outfitted by Opel in Europe. Caddy thinks that they'd only sell "a thousand or so." Duh.<P>------------------<BR>This should work until my site comes online....   <IMG SRC="http://homestead.deja.com/user.rshuman/files/Tmh.jpg"> ||||reg||1
